'''Sant Cristòfor International Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport Internacional de Sant Cristòfor}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: SCI, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: SCIB) is the busiest airport in [[Balboa]] and the principal international gateway to the [[Isthmus of Balboa]]. Located in the [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|ciutat of Sant Martí]] in eastern [[Sant Cristòfor]], the airport serves the metropolitan area of approximately 5.16 million people and the nearby [[International City of Areza]]. The airport is a hub for the flag carrier [[Balboa Airlines]] and the low-cost carrier [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]].

The airport is notable for its dual-code arrangement: Terminal D, a separate facility serving flights where all passengers are ticketed to the [[International City of Areza]], operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR). This arrangement, unique in Adonia, was established as part of the [[Adonian Community]]'s consolidation of air services to Areza following the closure of [[Areza International Airport]] in 2003.

== History ==

=== Planning and construction ===

The need for a new airport to serve Sant Cristòfor was identified in the early 1970s, as the existing airport at [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]] — which had been rebuilt in 1954 during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]] — was constrained by its urban location and could not be expanded to accommodate jet-era traffic. The [[Balboa Airports Authority]] was established in 1975 to oversee the planning, construction, and operation of the new facility.

A site in [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]] was selected in 1977, on flat agricultural land approximately 12 km east of the city centre. The site offered room for expansion, clear approaches over the coastal plain, and proximity to the planned Metro Line 1 extension. Construction began in 1979 and was completed in 1984; the airport opened on 15 March 1985.

=== Two-terminal era (1985–2000) ===

The airport opened with two terminals: Terminal A (domestic) and Terminal B (international, serving Abyalan and Lurandian routes). The two terminals were connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security, and baggage claim facilities. The initial capacity was approximately 12 million passengers per year.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 1]] was extended to the airport in 1986, providing a direct rail link to the city centre via the [[Grand Train Terminal]]. The airport rapidly exceeded its design capacity, handling 18 million passengers by 1995, and congestion in Terminal B became a persistent issue.

=== Terminal D and the Areza consolidation (2000–2003) ===

[[File:Terminal D Sant Cristofor Airport Areza placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminal D, the separate Areza terminal, with its own IATA code ARZ]]

The closure of [[Areza International Airport]] — the former [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] airport that had served the [[International City of Areza]] since 1954 — was mandated by the [[Adonian Community]] as part of the consolidation of Areza's transport infrastructure. The AC funded the construction of Terminal D at Sant Cristòfor International Airport as a replacement facility, with the requirement that all flights serving Areza be consolidated at the new terminal.

Terminal D opened in 2000 as a physically separate building approximately 800 metres south of the main terminal complex. It is connected to the main terminals by a covered walkway and a dedicated people mover. The terminal operates under the secondary IATA code '''ARZ''' (ICAO: SCAR), making Sant Cristòfor one of the few airports in Adonia with dual IATA codes. Any airline may operate ARZ-coded flights provided that all passengers on the flight are ticketed to Areza; passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear a dedicated Areza customs and immigration facility within Terminal D rather than Balboan customs, and are transported to Areza via the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service.

The closure of Areza International Airport was completed in 2003, when [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] — running from Sant Martí through the canal corridor to Areza — was opened. The Line 9 extension was built with bypass tracks at all stations, allowing express services to skip intermediate stops and provide a direct, non-stop connection between Terminal D and Areza in approximately 45 minutes.

=== Terminal C and modern expansion (2008–present) ===

Terminal C, a new international terminal serving flights to [[Kaftia]], [[Illypnia]], and [[Fosia]], opened in 2008. The terminal was built to relieve chronic congestion in Terminal B and to accommodate the growth of long-haul international traffic driven by Sant Cristòfor's expanding role as a financial hub. Terminal C is the largest of the four terminals and is connected to the main complex by an extension of the central atrium.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminals ===

[[File:Sant Cristofor Airport terminal layout placeholder.jpg|thumb|250px|Terminal layout of Sant Cristòfor International Airport]]

The airport has four terminals:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Terminal !! Opened !! Function !! Gates !! Annual capacity
|-
| A || 1985 || Domestic || 18 || ~10 million
|-
| B || 1985 || International (Abyala / Lurandia) || 22 || ~12 million
|-
| C || 2008 || International (Kaftia / Illypnia / Fosia) || 28 || ~15 million
|-
| D || 2000 || Areza services (ARZ code) || 8 || ~5 million
|-
| '''Total''' || || '''76''' || '''~42 million'''
|}

Terminals A, B, and C are connected by a central atrium with shared check-in, security screening, and baggage claim facilities on the landside, and post-security airside corridors linking the gate areas. Terminal D is a separate building approximately 800 metres south of the main complex, connected by a covered walkway and a people mover. Terminal D has its own dedicated check-in, security, customs, and baggage claim facilities; passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear [[International City of Areza|Areza]] customs and immigration rather than Balboan, and do not enter Balboan territory. This distinction is significant because Areza maintains a 15-day open visa policy for all [[Adonian Community]] member states, whereas Balboa applies its own separate visa requirements. Passengers travelling to Areza who would require a Balboan visa for transit through the main terminals can therefore use ARZ-coded flights without needing to meet Balboan immigration requirements.

=== Runways ===

The airport has two parallel runways:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Direction !! Length !! Surface !! Use
|-
| 09/27 || 3,200 m || Asphalt || Primary; all commercial operations
|-
| 15/33 || 2,600 m || Asphalt || Secondary; domestic, cargo, general aviation
|}

Runway 09/27 is the primary runway, handling the majority of commercial operations. Runway 15/33 is used for domestic services, cargo operations, and general aviation, and serves as a relief runway during peak periods. Both runways are equipped with [[Instrument Landing System|ILS]] Category II approach systems.

=== Cargo facilities ===

Sant Cristòfor International Airport is a major cargo hub, reflecting the city's position at the junction of the Illypnian, Fosian, and Kaftian markets and its proximity to the [[Balboa Canal]]. The airport handled 612,000 tonnes of cargo in 2025, making it the busiest cargo airport in the isthmus region. Cargo facilities are located on the eastern side of the airport, adjacent to Runway 15/33, with dedicated cargo aprons, warehousing, and logistics facilities operated by the [[Balboa Airports Authority]] and private logistics companies.

== Airlines and destinations ==

=== Hub operations ===

[[Balboa Airlines]], the national flag carrier, maintains its primary hub at the airport, operating domestic and international routes from Terminals A, B, and C. [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]], a low-cost carrier, operates domestic and short-haul international routes primarily from Terminal A.

=== ARZ-coded services ===

The ARZ code is used for flights where all passengers are ticketed to the [[International City of Areza]]. Any airline may operate ARZ-coded flights from Terminal D, provided that all passengers on the flight hold confirmed onward travel to Areza. In practice, ARZ-coded flights are operated primarily by the flag carriers of [[Adonian Community]] member states, reflecting the AC's role as the principal destination for diplomatic, institutional, and official travel to Areza. Passengers on ARZ-coded flights clear Areza customs and immigration within Terminal D and are transported to Areza via the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] express service, which operates non-stop between Terminal D station and Areza station in approximately 45 minutes. The ARZ arrangement eliminates the need for passengers travelling to Areza to clear Balboan customs or meet Balboan visa requirements, streamlining the connection between international flights and the AC institutions. This is of particular importance because Areza maintains a 15-day open visa policy for all [[Adonian Community]] member states, whereas Balboa applies its own separate visa requirements — meaning that nationals of AC member states who would require a Balboan visa for transit through the main terminals can travel to Areza via ARZ-coded flights without restriction.

== Ground transport ==

=== Metro ===

{{main|Metro de Sant Cristòfor}}

The airport is served by two lines of the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]]:

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Line 1]] connects the airport to the city centre via the [[Grand Train Terminal]], with a journey time of approximately 35 minutes. Line 1 serves all stations between the airport and the city centre.

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Line 9]] connects the airport to the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]] via the canal corridor, a distance of 51.2 km, with a journey time of approximately 45 minutes on the express service. Line 9 was built with bypass tracks at all intermediate stations, allowing express services to skip local stops and provide a direct connection between Terminal D and Areza. The express service operates every 10 minutes during peak hours and every 15 minutes off-peak; all-stations services operate on the same tracks at a lower frequency.

The airport metro station is located beneath the central atrium, with direct access to Terminals A, B, and C. A separate station, served only by Line 9 express services, is located adjacent to Terminal D.

=== Road ===

The airport is connected to the city road network via the [[Eastern Highway]] (B-1), which runs from the airport westward to the city centre. Journey time by road to Original is approximately 25–40 minutes depending on traffic. Taxi, ride-hailing, and private vehicle access is available at all terminals. Long-term and short-term parking is provided in multi-storey car parks adjacent to each terminal.

=== Bus ===

The [[Autoritat de Transport de Sant Cristòfor]] (ATS) operates airport bus routes connecting the airport to major hotels, the cruise terminal, and the ciutats not served by the Metro. Airport bus services depart from the ground transportation centre beneath the central atrium.

